## Part 4 — How n8n Connects to FastAPI

This is the most important part to understand.

### Inside Docker: Use the Service Name as the Hostname

When both containers are running inside the same `docker compose` stack, Docker creates a **private internal network** between them. Within this network, each container can reach the others using their **service name** as a hostname — NOT `localhost`.

So inside your n8n workflow, all HTTP Request nodes that call your FastAPI must use:

```
http://video-generator:8000/generate-video
http://video-generator:8000/upload-image
http://video-generator:8000/upload-voiceover
http://video-generator:8000/upload-music
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **`video-generator`** is the service name defined in `docker-compose.yml`. Docker automatically resolves this to the correct internal IP address of the FastAPI container. Never use `localhost:8000` inside n8n workflows when running in Docker — `localhost` inside the n8n container points to n8n itself, not your FastAPI!

### When Running on a Cloud/Remote Server

If your Docker stack is deployed on a remote server (e.g. a VPS, Railway, or Render), replace `video-generator` with the **public URL** of the FastAPI container:

```
https://your-server-ip:8000/generate-video
```

Or better, put it behind a reverse proxy (like Nginx or Caddy) and use a domain name:
```
https://api.yourdomain.com/generate-video
```

